  "response": "## Scope check first\n\nThe sweep has one target, not many. `crimes scan --format json` reports 39 findings; filtering on `\"type\": \"hardcoded_local_path\"` yields exactly **one**:\n\n| id | file:line | evidence (literal) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `crime_00018` | `src/api/files.ts:9` | `` `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` @L9 `` |\n\nA repo-wide grep for `/Users/<name>/`, `/home/<name>/`, and `C:\\Users\\<name>\\` across `.ts`, `.tsx`, `.json`, `.md` confirms the single hit at `src/api/files.ts:9` — the detector didn't miss a sibling. One caveat on \"every\": the scan's `coverage.warnings` records `.crimes/suppressions.json` as excluded and never read, so findings there are absent rather than zero. It's a scanner config file, so a baked-in dev path is unlikely, but it's unverified.\n\nSo this is a one-line fix, not a sweep. Below is the plan for that line, plus the one adjacent finding a portability pass would reasonably pick up.\n\n## The finding\n\n```ts\n// src/api/files.ts\nconst SCHEMA_PATH = \"/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json\";\n\nexport function loadPlanSchema(): unknown {\n  if (!existsSync(SCHEMA_PATH)) return null;\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nTwo things worth knowing before choosing a replacement:\n\n- The path points **outside** this repo. Repo root is `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/evals/fixtures/01-messy-ts-app`; the literal reaches up to `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/`. The schema is not repo content today.\n- `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` **does not exist** even on this machine. Combined with the `existsSync` guard, `loadPlanSchema()` returns `null` silently in every environment including the author's. The portability bug is currently masked by a swallowed miss, so nothing fails loudly — which is worse than a crash, because callers can't distinguish \"no schema configured\" from \"schema empty\".\n\n## Replacement: repo-relative, with an env override\n\n**Recommended anchor: a relative path resolved from the repo root**, with `process.env.PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH` as an optional override for deploys that mount the file elsewhere.\n\n```ts\nimport { fileURLToPath } from \"node:url\";\nimport { resolve } from \"node:path\";\n\n// package.json declares \"type\": \"module\", so no __dirname — resolve from this module's URL.\nconst REPO_ROOT = fileURLToPath(new URL(\"../../\", import.meta.url));\n\nconst SCHEMA_PATH = process.env.PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH\n  ? resolve(process.env.PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH)\n  : resolve(REPO_ROOT, \"schema/plan.schema.json\");\n```\n\nThis requires vendoring the schema into the repo at `schema/plan.schema.json`. That's the actual fix: a JSON schema the app validates against is app source, it should be versioned with the code that reads it, and then every developer, CI runner, and deploy gets it for free with no configuration at all. The env var exists only for the deploy that wants to inject a different schema revision — not as the primary mechanism.\n\n**Why not `os.homedir()`:** the home directory is the wrong anchor here, despite the literal starting with `/Users/andrew/`. Rewriting it as `path.join(os.homedir(), \"dev/crimes/schema.json\")` would just re-encode one developer's workspace layout in portable syntax — it still resolves to nothing on CI and nothing in production, because `~/dev/crimes/` is a personal checkout location, not a stable home-relative convention. `os.homedir()` is the right call when the file genuinely belongs to the invoking user (`~/.config/<app>/`, a credentials cache, a per-user log). A shared schema definition is not that.\n\n**Why `process.env` alone is second-best:** making `PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH` mandatory pushes a required setup step onto every developer and every CI job for a file that never varies between environments. It converts a missing-file bug into a missing-config bug. Keep the env var as an override, not the source of truth.\n\n**One behavior change to make alongside it:** replace the silent `existsSync → null` with a thrown error (or a discriminated result) when the resolved path is missing. Otherwise the vendored-schema fix can regress back to returning `null` everywhere and no test will notice.\n\n## Adjacent, related but a different type\n\n`crime_00017` (`hardcoded_localhost`, medium) sits on the line immediately above, in the same const block:\n\n```ts\nconst ADMIN_DASHBOARD = \"http://localhost:3000/admin\";  // evidence: `localhost:3000` @L8\n```\n\nThis is a URL, not a filesystem path, so it's outside what you asked me to plan — but it's the same two-line block and any portability edit touches it. The replacement there is genuinely `process.env`: `process.env.ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL` with the `localhost:3000` value moved to `.env.example`, since a dashboard host really does differ per environment. Neither `os.homedir()` nor repo-relative resolution applies to a URL.\n\nI left this as a note rather than folding it into the plan — say the word and I'll write both fixes.",
